Excellent — the LearnOpenGL reference is exactly the canonical rule set:
1. Reposition the ball outside the AABB (penetration-based push-out).
2. Direction = the face of collision; reverse the matching velocity component.
3. Their direction detection uses the closest-point (dot product / compass) method.

Note: their corner handling — they pick the direction via the closest point's offset (the compass direction nearest to the diff vector). Interesting: their method effectively picks the axis of least penetration (via the closest point). They don't explicitly handle the exact corner (equal distances) — but the diff vector direction handles it: if the closest point is exactly the corner, the diff vector is diagonal, and the compass picks the dominant axis; a tie → their code picks the first max (index order: up, right, down, left — with `>` not `>=`, the FIRST direction with the max dot wins). My implementation handles the exact tie as a corner (flip both) — which is the strictest interpretation of the skill's family table ("les deux vitesses sont inversées").

I have what I need from the searches:
- Tunneling = discrete detection limitation; fix = swept (continuous) detection between positions. (bugnet.io, unityqueen.com, GameDev.net swept AABB.)
- Ghost collisions at seams = adjacent colliders + discrete sampling; fix = handle one collision per pass, prefer the earliest entry. (Brian Semrau's blog, Godot/godot forums.)
- Resolution = least-penetration axis (team-nutshell, LearnOpenGL), reposition + reverse the matching axis (LearnOpenGL).

